title: Example - Markdown link - inline formatting
type: text/vnd.tiddlywiki;vocab=Markdown

**Pattern:** `` [**bold** *italic* `code`](#target) `` — emphasis and other inline markers fire inside the link text.

The bracketed body of a link parses as an inline run (because [LINK marker tiddler](#vocab/markdown/LINK) doesn't set `body-attribute`), so any inline marker that's active in the current vocab fires inside it. The captured target still becomes the link destination.

**Live example:**

[A **bold** link with *italic* and `code`](#Example - Markdown)

[Visit **TiddlyWiki** today!](https://tiddlywiki.com)

And the inverse — a link can sit inside other emphasis:

**Visit the [home page](#Example - Markdown) any time.**

**Source:**

```
[A **bold** link with *italic* and `code`](#Example - Markdown)

[Visit **TiddlyWiki** today!](https://tiddlywiki.com)

**Visit the [home page](#Example - Markdown) any time.**
```

**Why this works:** the LINK marker has no `body-attribute` set, so the engine treats the body as inline content. `parseInlineRun` runs the full inline-rules set on the captured body text, which includes the markdown vocab's BOLD, ITALIC, CODE, etc. Result: rich inline content as the rendered link's children.

**Caveat:** a nested LINK inside link text (`[[outer](u1)](u2)` or `[outer [inner](u1)](u2)`) is matched greedily by the regex — the first `]` terminates the outer body. CommonMark forbids nested links anyway; if you need one, restructure the text.

